    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. However, looking at the practical application in recent Supreme Court jurisprudence, it seems like Originalism provides a much more rigorous, text-based framework that actually constrains judicial discretion. Doesn't Living Constitutionalism essentially just provide a legal veneer for judges to implement their own policy preferences under the guise of \"evolving standards\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalism is a judicial philosophy that insists on interpreting the Constitution as it was understood at the time of its framing. This approach is rooted in a belief that the Constitution is a finite document, and that the role of the judiciary is to apply its text as it was originally intended, not to adapt it to modern circumstances. Proponents argue that this method respects the rule of law and the separation of powers, as it limits the judiciary's role to that of an interpreter rather than a lawmaker. They contend that the framers' intentions are the ultimate guide, and that any deviation from this undermines the democratic process and the Constitution's legitimacy.\n\n\nIn contrast, Living Constitutionalism is criticized by Originalists as a form of judicial activism that allows judges to impose their own values and policy preferences onto the Constitution. This approach is seen as inherently unstable and unpredictable, as it relies on subjective interpretations of what the framers would have thought about contemporary issues. Critics argue that this method effectively grants judges the power to legislate from the bench, which is antithetical to the principles of a limited government and checks and balances. They believe that the Constitution should be a stable, enduring document that provides clear guidance, rather than a malleable tool that can be",
      "source": "target",
